      "content": "International organizations often fail to protect specific national interests because they are structurally designed to prioritize collective consensus over individual demands, forcing nations to yield political leverage to avoid deadlock. These institutions undermine sovereignty by establishing supranational norms and enforcement mechanisms that bypass domestic legal systems, effectively allowing member states to agree to cede certain decision-making powers in exchange for security or economic benefits. Ultimately, the very requirement for unanimity or broad majority agreement allows powerful states or veto wields to block the very interventions needed to shield vulnerable nations from external crisis.",
